\section{Problem Setup and Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Safety Commitment Point}
\label{sec:commitment-point}

When a reasoning model is jailbroken, its chain-of-thought (CoT) transitions from safety-aware deliberation to committed harmful generation. We define the \textbf{Safety Commitment Point} (CP) as the first reasoning step after which safety reasoning permanently ceases; traces with oscillating safety deliberation are resolved by taking the final irreversible transition (\cref{sec:limitations}). Steps before CP are \emph{pre-commitment} ($y_t = 0$); steps at or after CP are \emph{post-commitment} ($y_t = 1$). The detection task produces a score $s_t \in [0, 1]$ estimating whether the model has passed its CP by step $t$.

We study four reasoning models spanning two architectures and two scales (\cref{tab:models}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Arch.} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{Layers} & \textbf{Hidden} & \textbf{$n$ (total\,/\,test)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & Llama & 8B & 32 & 4096 & 209\,/\,43 \\
OT-7B & Qwen & 7B & 28 & 3584 & 228\,/\,47 \\
R1-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 100\,/\,20 \\
QwQ-32B & Qwen & 32B & 64 & 5120 & 95\,/\,19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Models studied. R1-8B and R1-32B are distilled from DeepSeek-R1~\citep{deepseekr1}; QwQ-32B~\citep{qwq} is a native reasoning model; OT-7B~\citep{openthinker} is trained with STILL~\citep{still} on Qwen2.5-7B. Data: HarmThoughts~\citep{harmthoughts} with step-level CP annotations. Split: 60/20/20.}
\label{tab:models}
\end{table}

\subsection{Probe Architecture}
\label{sec:probe-architecture}

\paragraph{Hidden-state (HS) probes.} At each step $t$, we extract the last-token hidden state $\mathbf{h}_t^{(\ell)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ from layer $\ell$ and train an L2-regularized logistic regression classifier (balanced class weights, $C{=}1.0$, LBFGS, max\_iter${}=2000$) on binary CP labels. Sweeping $C \in [0.01, 100]$ yields $<$2pp BA variation (\cref{sec:experiments}), confirming robustness to this default. Layer selection is analyzed in \cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}.

\paragraph{Text probes.} Each step's text is encoded with all-MiniLM-L6-v2~\citep{wang2020minilm} ($\mathbb{R}^{384}$), a distilled sentence encoder from the Sentence-BERT family~\citep{reimers2019sentence}. The same logistic regression is applied as a surface-level modality baseline.

\paragraph{Temporal windowing.} For window size $W$, we concatenate features from steps $[t{-}W{+}1, \ldots, t]$. We evaluate $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ and select per-model on validation. GRU sequence models are also evaluated under two protocols: a 70/30 train/test split (primary) and the same 60/20/20 split used for LR experiments (for fair comparison; \cref{tab:gru_multi_seeds,app:gru_details}).

\subsection{Layer Selection Sensitivity}
\label{sec:layer-sensitivity}

Layer selection is a researcher degree of freedom that can reverse modality comparison conclusions. We evaluate five metrics: balanced accuracy (BA), precision, and inverse FPR are standard probing metrics~\citep{belinkov2022probing}; crossing rate and additive composite come from the safety detection literature. A multiplicative composite (BA $\times$ crossing rate) serves as the \emph{crossing-rate composite} (CR-composite) baseline, so named because it incorporates crossing rate in the selection criterion. Metrics evaluating probe performance at a fixed decision threshold (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) form the \emph{threshold-based} selection. This classification is an empirical observation from metric disagreement, not an a priori design choice. Crossing rate inflates at shallow layers where representations are compressed (inter-step cosine distances 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller; \cref{app:cosine_volatility}), an independently verifiable geometric property. Precision, inverse FPR, and additive composite all derive from the same $2\times2$ confusion matrix and empirically converge to the same layer, consistent with their shared structure but not mathematically guaranteed (each metric defines a distinct optimization landscape over layers); BA is threshold-free and provides independent confirmation. The ablation diagnoses which \emph{aspect} drives layer selection bias. The effective number of independent tests is four (one per model), reflected in our HB correction. Results in \cref{sec:layer-results}; full per-layer sweeps in \cref{app:layer_sweep}.

\subsection{Baseline Methods}
\label{sec:baselines}

We evaluate seven alternatives to contextualize probe performance (\cref{tab:negative}): BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} and CUSUM~\citep{page1954cusum} for change-point detection; Mahalanobis distance following TrajGuard~\citep{trajguard}; RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation}; two-layer MLP probes; CCS-adapted~\citep{burns2023discovering}; and cross-model transfer via PCA. A position-only baseline ($t/T$) controls for temporal artifacts. Detailed configurations in \cref{app:bocpd_config,app:transfer_details}.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

We report balanced accuracy, precision, pre-CP FPR, coverage, and lead time. We exclude F1 (conflates precision and recall), AUROC (threshold-free, cannot diagnose layer selection bias), and MCC (redundant with BA under balanced priors). Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings, selected on the R1-8B validation set and held fixed across all models as the value balancing coverage ($\geq$70\%) and safe-trace false positive rate ($\leq$50\%); \cref{tab:k_ablation} confirms that the HS vs.\ text conclusion direction is consistent at $K{=}3$, $5$, and $7$, with coverage and FPR trading off monotonically. Modality comparisons use BCa bootstrap (10{,}000 resamples, resampling traces as clusters: each trace is an independent unit and all its steps are included or excluded together, preserving within-trace dependence)~\citep{efron1993bootstrap,diciccio1996bootstrap} with Holm-Bonferroni step-down (3 metrics $\times$ 4 models $= 12$ tests, $\alpha{=}0.05$); the 4-test framework yields identical conclusions (\cref{app:holm_bonferroni}). For 32B models ($n \leq 20$), the exact permutation test is the primary inference method; BCa confidence intervals are reported for comparability but may have reduced coverage at these sample sizes (BCa assumes approximate pivotality; heavy-tailed score distributions could produce anti-conservative intervals). Within each model, the three metrics are positively correlated, so the correction is stricter than under independence. Control task selectivity~\citep{hewitt2019control} validates genuine structure (\cref{app:control}). $\dagger$ marks sample-limited test sets ($n \leq 20$): R1-32B ($n{=}20$) and QwQ-32B ($n{=}19$).

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}.

\textit{Attack characterization.} Self-jailbreak has been documented with step-level annotations~\cite{harmthoughts}, with longer chains increasing circumvention probability~\cite{selfjailbreak}. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful,turpin2023language} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. Safety-aligned models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience a sharp drop at output generation~\cite{yin2025refusal_cliff,hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak,hu2026refusal_dynamics,ahrend2026safer_traces}.

\textit{Detection.} CoT monitoring can detect reward hacking but optimization pressure promotes obfuscation~\cite{baker2025monitoring}. CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile~\cite{korbak2025cot_monitorability}. GuardReasoner~\cite{xiang2025guardreasoner} trains a dedicated reasoning-based guard model.

\textit{Mitigation.} SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and Chain-of-Guardrails~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} proposes guardrail insertion during reasoning. None of these conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{azaria2023internal} demonstrate that internal states encode whether a model's output is truthful, motivating representation-based monitoring. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, \citet{turner2023activation} steer behavior via activation addition without optimization, and \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, \citet{templeton2024scaling} extract interpretable features at scale, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, consistent with our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection; HiddenDetect~\cite{jiang2025hiddendetect} extends hidden-state monitoring to vision-language models; SafeInt~\cite{safeint2025} applies representation intervention for defense; RCS~\cite{rcs2026} uses Mahalanobis-based contrastive scoring for jailbreak detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} decompose internal layer representations to separate jailbreak from benign prompts; their results confirm that layer-wise structure carries safety-relevant signal. Concurrently, \citet{chan2025probing} probe safety commitment in LLMs, complementing our focus on metric-induced layer selection bias. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Causal abstraction~\cite{geiger2025causal} formalizes when high-level causal models faithfully describe neural computation, grounding use of internal representations. Linear probing spans layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding}, sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing}, and surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing,rogers2020primer}. Control tasks~\cite{hewitt2019control} distinguish structure from memorization (we adopt these; \cref{app:control}). Information-theoretic and causal formulations address probe complexity~\cite{voita2020information,pimentel2020information,ravfogel2020null,ravfogel2022rlace,belrose2023leace,elazar2021amnesic}. \citet{marks2024geometry} find emergent linear structure in LLM truth representations, consistent with our safety commitment separability. That information distributes non-uniformly across layers is well established~\cite{tenney2019bert,jawahar2019bert}. Our contribution is orthogonal: the known layer-wise information gradient interacts with metric choice to produce systematic selection bias in safety probes. Most directly, \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations; our metric bias analysis provides a complementary mechanism: shallow-layer probes exploit compressed representations that inflate crossing rate, a geometric effect consistent with text-leakage at layers where representations have not yet diverged from surface features. \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} apply hidden-state geometry to step-level hallucination detection concurrently with our work. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis, a practice we show can reverse conclusions (\cref{sec:layer-results}). \citet{pais2026linear_probe_scaling} show that multi-layer ensembling recovers performance where single-layer probes fail, a complementary solution to the sensitivity problem we diagnose. Our approach is correlational; causal validation~\citep{geiger2025causal,belrose2023leace,li2024iti} is needed to confirm probed directions are causally active.

\paragraph{Alternative Detection Approaches.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B). These failures motivate supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.

